-- title:
--   Stalks of the special fibre of a finite-type morphism are noetherian
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative local ring, with no noetherian hypothesis imposed, let $X$ be a scheme, let $f : X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ be a morphism which is locally of finite type, and let $x$ be a point of $X$ whose image under the underlying continuous map of $f$ is the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Write $c : A \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ for the structural map of constants, spelled out as the composite of the inverse of the isomorphism $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A, \mathcal{O}) \cong A$, the map $f^{\sharp}$ on global sections $\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A,\mathcal{O}) \to \Gamma(X,\mathcal{O}_X)$, and the germ map $\Gamma(X,\mathcal{O}_X) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ at $x$. The conclusion is that the quotient ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x} / \mathfrak{m}_A\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$, where the ideal divided out is the ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ generated by the image under $c$ of the maximal ideal of $A$, is a noetherian ring.
--
--   This is the standard fact that the fibre of a morphism locally of finite type over a field is locally noetherian, applied to the special fibre $X \times_{\operatorname{Spec} A} \operatorname{Spec}(A/\mathfrak{m}_A)$ and read off at a stalk; it matters because $A$ itself may be a non-noetherian valuation ring, as for the bases of semistable models. It is used in the analysis of nodal curves, where it discharges the noetherianity hypothesis on the ring of reductions at a point.
-- source:
